What in this package is Anthropic's, and what is third-party
-------------------------------------------------------------
Anthropic's own work, licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (see LICENSE) OR the MIT licence (see LICENSE-MIT), at the recipient's option — each such source and documentation file carries the header 'SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 OR MIT', except the proof files that restate instruction words of the task's compiled programs, or transcribe or derive data from those programs, which begin with a one-line provenance comment instead, and the data and index files (JSON, CSV, the checksum list, the decoded companions), which carry no header: the proof trees (libraries/*/proofs/**, Rocq sources) — apart from the third-party object code that some proof files transcribe as data, which keeps its own licences (class 3 below), and apart from the files copied or adapted from Theorem's shared proof library (class 4 below) — the classifier source files, the model-edition descriptions, and all documentation (README, INDEX, MANIFEST, docs/, the per-library README files). The top-level statement file of each tree (AgentSubmission/Proof.v), which reproduces the theorem statements issued by Theorem, the files adapted from Theorem's shared proof library, and the model-edition diffs, which quote lines of Theorem's model sources (class 4 below), carry the same header and are offered under the same choice of licences, Theorem having confirmed in writing that its material in this package may be distributed under either.

Third-party material falls in four classes:
  1. One or more licence/notice files of each upstream open-source project, reproduced verbatim under libraries/<name>/. No other upstream source code is included, except class 2.
  2. Three fuzz-harness source files taken unmodified from the google/oss-fuzz project (Apache-2.0).
  3. Object code. Many proof files transcribe, as data, the RISC-V program images that the proofs are about. Those images were built by the task's authors from: the upstream library at its pinned commit; the harness; the musl C library, statically linked; LLVM's compiler-rt (the runtime library of every build and, in the AddressSanitizer builds, the AddressSanitizer runtime; the UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer builds use inline traps and no runtime) and LLVM's libunwind, both on every link line; for the C++ entry points, LLVM's libc++ and libc++abi C++ runtime, statically linked together with libunwind; and the task authors' own command-line wrapper and harness glue; for some entry points also further open-source libraries that the upstream project bundles in its tree or that its build links from the toolchain's system root, named in that library's section below. The licences of those components apply to those bytes. Some proof trees also embed the program image of this package's own input-classifier program, built from its source here with the same musl C library and LLVM compiler-rt runtime (in one tree, with compiler-rt's UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer runtime as well); the musl and LLVM notices below cover those bytes. Each proof tree's SUBMISSION.json lists the files that carry program images or code transcriptions.
  4. Materials of Theorem, the task's authors, that the package includes or quotes — the theorem statements they issued (reproduced as issued in each tree's Proof.v), model-edition diffs quoting their model sources, module and statement names, and their shared 'Common' proof library: a set of generic Rocq lemma files (symbolic-execution tactics, process start-up, allocator and stream models — for example StepTac.v, MmapSim.v, ExitSim.v and the LibcStart start-up files) that the task image delivers for proof authors to copy into their submissions and extend. Files copied or adapted from that library appear in most trees under AgentSubmission/ (typically in a Common/ folder), and the object code of Theorem's command-line wrapper, harness adapter and C-library stand-ins is among the bytes transcribed in class 3; Theorem has confirmed in writing the licence under which its material here may be distributed (see the opening paragraph); the copyright in that material is Theorem's, and the uniform 'Copyright 2026 Anthropic PBC' header line on the adapted files covers Anthropic's modifications, not Theorem's underlying text. Other files delivered alongside that library originate in Anthropic's automated proving runs — Theorem's own sources mark them as adopted from 'the collaborators' — and are treated here as Anthropic's work.

